# Failed driver setup teardown (issue 150)

Base: `b0a5699b34e18903c5243dc9884b4ec1c960f4cd`.
Scope: shared conformance lifecycle and its inert regression only. No driver,
rollback, provider, authentication, renderer or source-identity code changes.

The suite assigns its handle only after the factory resolves. When startup
throws or rejects, afterAll still runs, and `handle.dispose?.()` dereferences
the uninitialized handle before optional access can help. It adds
`TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'dispose')` to the
real setup error. The handle type now includes undefined and teardown uses
`await handle?.dispose?.()`. Setup still rejects with its original error;
an actual returned handle's disposer is still awaited and may still fail.

The new control transforms and executes the real conformance module using
the existing Vite/Oxc transformer, with inert registration hooks. It invokes
the actual beforeAll/afterAll callbacks without running SQL bodies or starting
a provider. Five cases cover thrown startup, rejected startup, no disposer,
awaited single disposal and visible disposer failure. Both failed-startup
cases assert the identical original error and no disposal of an unreturned
resource. Before repair: two failed, three passed, zero skipped; both failures
are the exact undefined-handle dispose TypeError. After repair: all five pass.
The first baseline used Node type stripping; the final control reuses the
project's existing transformer. Its lifecycle assertions are unchanged.

Final focused verification: 16 tests pass in three files, zero failed/skipped:
the five inert controls plus the existing file-backed LocalSqliteDriver six
tests and @libsql/client file-backed cross-check five tests. Ordinary transform
caches may be used; local database checks execute on isolated fresh files.
Typecheck exits zero. Named-file platform scanner: zero command hits.
Whitespace check passes. No new model call, installation, binary fetch,
native MySQL probe or server was started. Missing-provider failures and
exclusions remain unchanged; native/provider acceptance is not established.

